First, a quick summary of what’s going on and why it feels broken:

1.  Bug scenario  
    - When the number of tabs exceeds the width of their container, Next’s `Tab` component falls back to “slide” mode and renders only “prev/next” buttons — there is no native scrollbar and, by default, no dragging or keyboard navigation.  
    - The user wants to be able to use their mouse (wheel or drag) or their left/right arrows (or up/down arrows) to scroll through tabs.

2.  Root cause  
    - The sliding logic only exposes two buttons (`onPrevClick`/`onNextClick`) and never hooks up any wheel or keyboard events.  
    - As a result there is no way to use the mouse wheel (or drag) nor the arrow keys to move the tab list.

3.  The fix  
    - Hook the `<div className="tabs-nav-container">` up to a new `onWheel` handler that calls the existing `setOffset` logic.  
    - Hook the same container up to an `onKeyDown` handler that watches for left/right (or up/down) arrow key codes and calls `onPrevClick`/`onNextClick`.  
    - Fall back to any consumer-passed `onKeyDown` prop afterwards, so existing usage isn’t broken.

